      title: "Scripture",
      body: `## The primary source

For all Christian traditions, the primary source of theology is **Scripture**: the Bible, consisting of the Old and New Testaments. Christians believe that in the Scriptures God has spoken, and that the Bible is in some sense the "word of God." What exactly this means, and how the Bible should be interpreted, are questions on which Christians have disagreed.

## Which Bible?

Christians do not all have the same Bible. Protestants accept the thirty-nine books of the Hebrew Bible as their Old Testament. Roman Catholics also accept seven further books, the **deuterocanonical** books—Tobit, Judith, 1 and 2 Maccabees, Wisdom of Solomon, Sirach (Ecclesiasticus), and Baruch—along with additions to Esther and Daniel, which were part of the Greek Septuagint used by the early church. Protestants call these books the **Apocrypha**, and generally regard them as useful but not authoritative. Eastern Orthodox churches accept a somewhat larger collection still. All accept the same twenty-seven books of the New Testament, whose formation is studied in BIB 110.

## Inspiration

Christians have generally held that Scripture is **inspired** by God. The classic text is 2 Timothy 3:16: "All scripture is given by inspiration of God, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness." The Greek word translated "given by inspiration of God" is *theopneustos*, literally "God-breathed."

Theories of inspiration vary. Some early and medieval writers spoke of the human authors as instruments of the Holy Spirit, like a pen in a writer's hand or a lyre played by a musician. Most theologians, however, have insisted that the human authors were genuine authors, whose personalities, styles, and circumstances shaped their writing, even as God worked through them. The Second Vatican Council's *Dei Verbum* (1965), for example, described God as choosing human authors who "made use of their powers and abilities," so that they wrote as "true authors."

## Inerrancy and authority

A related question concerns **inerrancy**: whether the Bible is without error. Some Christians, especially in conservative evangelical traditions, hold that the Bible is without error in everything it affirms, including history and science, as stated in the *Chicago Statement on Biblical Inerrancy* (1978). Others hold that the Bible is without error in matters of faith and morals, the things it intends to teach for salvation, but may reflect the limited scientific and historical knowledge of its authors. *Dei Verbum* says that the Scriptures teach "firmly, faithfully and without error that truth which God wanted put into sacred writings for the sake of salvation"—a phrase interpreted in different ways. Still others, especially in liberal Protestant traditions, regard the Bible as a human witness to revelation rather than revelation itself.

Whatever their view of inerrancy, all Christian traditions regard Scripture as **authoritative**: as the norm by which Christian belief and life are to be measured.

## Interpretation

The Bible must be interpreted, and interpretation is never simple. The Bible contains many genres—history, law, poetry, prophecy, wisdom, gospel, letter, apocalypse—each requiring a different kind of reading. It was written over a thousand years, in ancient languages, in cultures very different from ours. Its parts sometimes seem to disagree. Christians have developed various principles of interpretation:

- reading **Scripture by Scripture**, interpreting obscure passages by clearer ones;
- the **literal sense** (what the human author meant) and **spiritual senses** (what God means through the text, especially in relation to Christ), which medieval interpreters divided into allegorical, moral, and anagogical (concerning the last things);
- the **rule of faith**: interpreting Scripture in harmony with the church's central beliefs, as summarized in the creeds;
- the **rule of love**: Augustine's principle, which we met in PAT 110, that any interpretation that builds up love of God and neighbor is consistent with Scripture's purpose;
- **historical-critical** methods, developed since the eighteenth century, which study the texts in their historical contexts.

## The Bible and Christ

For Christians, the center of Scripture is **Jesus Christ**. Jesus himself, according to the Gospels, said that the Scriptures "testify of me" (John 5:39), and Luke describes the risen Christ explaining to his disciples "in all the scriptures the things concerning himself" (Luke 24:27). Martin Luther called the Bible "the manger in which Christ is laid." Karl Barth, the great Swiss Reformed theologian of the twentieth century, distinguished three forms of the "Word of God": Christ, the Word made flesh; Scripture, which witnesses to him; and preaching, which proclaims him. On this view, the Bible's authority derives from the Christ to whom it witnesses.

> **Pause and consider.** Why might Christians who agree that the Bible is authoritative still disagree about what it teaches? What role does interpretation play?`,
